          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c is 58.",
          "answer": "I can't tell you what an HbA1c of 58 means from the guidance I found. The passages returned did not include the diagnostic cut-offs or a target for this number, and they did not say which units the result is in. Your lab report should state the units, and your clinician can explain what the value means for you.\n\nHere is what the retrieved guidance does say:\n\n- **Targets are individual.** The glycemic-targets summary says blood sugar is one of several goals, and that the HbA1c goal can be relaxed when severe low blood sugar is a risk. (*Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals*)\n- **The number alone doesn't decide treatment.** Current first-line choices depend on other conditions the person has, and for several of them the choice is made independently of the HbA1c value. (*Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management*)\n- **Abnormal results are usually rechecked.** Where two different tests are done and only one is above threshold, the usual approach is to repeat the abnormal one. (*Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals*)\n\nThe screening document, which should contain the diagnostic thresholds, was listed but its text did not come back in these results, so I can't quote them here. Please ask your clinician what your result means, whether it needs repeating, and what your personal target is.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
