          "question": "What code would that be recorded under?",
          "answer": "I can't name the specific code. The guidance I found doesn't list one for an HbA1c result.\n\nWhat it does say is that an abnormal lab result with no diagnosis recorded is usually filed in the symptoms and abnormal findings chapter, ICD-10-CM Chapter 18 (R00–R99). The endocrine chapter says an isolated raised glucose result with no diagnosis is classified there, not in the diabetes block. The chapter overview places abnormal blood findings at R70–R79. (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 18* and *ICD-10-CM Chapter 4: endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases*)\n\nCodes are assigned by the clinician or medical coder who records the visit, based on the full record. A code is a billing and record-keeping label, not a diagnosis. If you want to know how your result is recorded, ask your clinician or the lab or billing office.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
